What the licence change does and does not do
============================================

Cairn was released under the MIT License from its first commit through v0.5.1.
From commit 303a9f9, merged 2026-09-19 at 20:18 UTC, it is licensed under the
Sustainable Use License, in the file LICENSE beside this one.

The boundary is that commit. CONTRIBUTING.md cites the same one, because this
file used to draw the line at a tag and that one used to draw it at a date, and
those are not the same line.

A licence change is not retroactive, and nobody should read it as one.

  - Every release up to and including v0.5.1 remains MIT, permanently, for
    anyone who already has it or obtains it later. That cannot be withdrawn.
  - The git history remains MIT. A fork taken from any commit up to that tag
    carries the MIT terms with it.
  - Only new work, from the next release onward, is under the new licence.

Why
---

Cairn is going to have a hosted offering alongside the code you can run
yourself. MIT permits anyone to take this software and sell exactly that hosted
service, including in competition with the people who wrote it, and that is a
poor foundation to fund the work from.

The Sustainable Use License keeps what matters about an open project: you can
read the source, run it yourself for your own business or personally, modify it,
fork it, and share those changes. What it stops is one specific thing — selling
Cairn itself as a service.

This is the licence n8n publishes and uses, and the change is made for the same
reason they made it.

Contributions
-------------

Contributions made before this change were made under MIT and are acknowledged
as such. Contributions from this point are made under the Sustainable Use
License; CONTRIBUTING.md says so.

A pull request opened before 303a9f9 was offered under MIT, whenever it merges.
Its author read the licence that was in the repository at the time, and cannot
be held to one that arrived while the branch was open. MIT permits
sublicensing, so such a contribution ships inside this work under the
Sustainable Use License with its MIT-era authorship acknowledged here; the
contribution itself also remains available under MIT to anyone who takes it
from that pull request, and that is the correct outcome rather than a loophole.

  - #43, "Trust the proxy hop when checking a browser mutation's origin",
    opened 2026-09-19 at 19:57 UTC by Thierry Thiers, twenty-one minutes before
    303a9f9, and merged after it. Offered under MIT. He was asked on that pull
    request whether he would rather it were under the Sustainable Use License,
    and he answered yes — recorded below.

The MIT contributions were asked about, and answered
----------------------------------------------------

Two people other than the maintainer hold copyright in code that is still live
in this tree. Both were asked rather than assumed about, and both answered.

`e09b1cd`, "fix: make messages inbox readable at a glance", contributed
2026-04-03 by jgiffard. Checking rather than assuming before the licence moved
showed it is not dead code: 160 of its lines are still live —
`src/lib/message-preview.ts` untouched at 80 of 80, plus most of its test and
part of `src/components/compact-markdown-preview.tsx`. Not de minimis, so it was
the one earlier contribution the relicence genuinely turned on.

We had been told he would be fine with it and proceeded on that. Nobody had
asked him. He was asked on #55 and #61, and answered on
[#61](https://github.com/montytorr/cairn/pull/61) on 2026-09-21:

    I also confirm that my past and future contributions to Cairn may be
    licensed under the Sustainable Use Licence.

It is written down here rather than left in the thread, because the point of
asking was to replace an assumption with a record, and a record that exists only
inside a merged pull request is not one. This does not withdraw the MIT grant
already made in that commit, which cannot be withdrawn; it means the Sustainable
Use License version of those lines rests on their author's word rather than on
someone else's account of it.

Thierry Thiers holds three commits, all offered under MIT: `154549b` and
`a9e99a8` from 2026-09-13, and `92aa720` — #43 — opened at 19:57 UTC on
2026-09-19, twenty-one minutes before `303a9f9`, and merged after it.

Measured against HEAD with git blame, this is the larger of the two holdings:
roughly 400 live lines, most of them `154549b` — the project-repos feature, its
route, its tests, and `migrations/030_project_repos.sql`, which is the same file
renumbered when the migrations moved out of `supabase/`.

He was asked on #43, in terms that named all three commits, and answered there
on 2026-09-21:

    Answering the outstanding question in LICENSE-MIT-HISTORY: yes — I am happy
    for this contribution to be under the project's licence, the Sustainable Use
    License, alongside everything else in Cairn. Please record it as settled
    rather than outstanding.

    [...] I consent to #43 being used and distributed under the Sustainable Use
    License as part of Cairn. I make no attempt to withdraw the MIT offer
    already made — it was made, and anyone who took it from the pull request
    keeps it.

Both sentences are quoted because their scope is not identical. The question he
answered "yes" to named all three commits; the belt-and-braces sentence after it
names #43. This file reads the yes as covering all three, and states that
reading here rather than making it quietly, so that he can correct it if he
meant the narrower thing. His MIT offer stands in either case and nothing here
withdraws it.

If you contributed to Cairn under MIT and would rather your work were not
relicensed, say so in an issue and it will be honoured. If you would rather it
were relicensed, say so on your pull request and it will be recorded there.
